$DIR/main.p:20:9: cannot return fmt.Sprintf("item %d", n), which points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of name
    fmt.Sprintf("item %d", n) points into memory allocated from a, a local arena
    of name. alloc.Use(&a) (at main.p:19) makes a (a local arena of name) the
    default allocator, so what is allocated through the default allocator from
    there on (by new, a string operation, or a call that allocates and leaves
    out its allocator) lives in the arena, and is gone when the arena is. When
    name returns, the arena a is gone, so fmt.Sprintf("item %d", n) would point
    at memory that is no longer allocated.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Allocate what must outlive the arena from an allocator that does, passed
    explicitly:
        heap := alloc.Default() // before alloc.Use
        ...
        v := vec.New[T](heap)
    Or install an arena that outlives the result, such as one the caller passes
    in, or make the copy that must outlive the arena after the Restore is
    dropped.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:29:9: cannot return v, which points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of items
    v points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of items. alloc.Use(&a)
    (at main.p:26) makes a (a local arena of items) the default allocator, so
    what is allocated through the default allocator from there on (by new, a
    string operation, or a call that allocates and leaves out its allocator)
    lives in the arena, and is gone when the arena is. When items returns, the
